  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nA shortwave disturbance over northeastern Iowa will continue to\ndrive showers and embedded thunderstorms eastward through early\nthis evening, with the greatest coverage expected north of \nI-80. While a 20% chance for lightning remains, poor mid-level \nlapse rates across northern Illinois should limit thunderstorms \nto isolated coverage.\n\nFollowing the eastward departure of this short-wave impulse \nthis evening, a southwesterly low-level jet will ramp up again \novernight, with the core expected to reside over IA \nnortheastward into southern WI. Associated isentropic upglide \n(warm air advection) may thus foster more isolated to scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms late tonight into early Monday \nmorning. Thereafter, capping is likely to curtail the threat of \nconvection for much of the area into Monday afternoon. However, \nanother short-wave impulse is progged to translate eastward \ntowards northwestern IL late in the day, and thus may act as the\nimpetus for isolated to widely scattered afternoon development \nacross northwestern IL after 2 pm. Should this activity occur, \n~40 kt of mid-level westerly flow could support a few stronger \nstorms across northwestern IL late in the afternoon. Confidence \nremains low with the extent of this threat. \n\nConvective trends continue to be of low confidence Monday night\ninto Tuesday morning. Nevertheless, low (20-30%)chances for \nstorms continue due to the persistent warm-air advection pattern\nin combination with additional smaller scale perturbations \nlikely to transition eastward across southern WI through the \nmid-level ridge axis. The better focus for storms may remain in \nsouthern WI, but at this time, cannot rule out storms as far \nsouth as the I-80 corridor. The SPC day 3 severe outlook (for \nTuesday) places most areas north of I-80 in a level 1 of 5 \nthreat. So, while confidence in the coverage of storms remains \nlow into Tuesday morning, the meteorological environment will be\nsupportive of a conditional threat for strong storms. \n\nOutside of the threat of storms through Tuesday morning, \ntemperatures and humidity levels will be on the rise through \nmid-week as the airmass under the expansive mid/upper-level \nridge continues to warm. We anticipate high temperatures in the \nupper 80s Monday, the low 90s Tuesday and the low to mid 90s \nWednesday. In spite of us being past the peak timing for \nevapotranspiration, limited mixing under an inversion centered \nnear 850 mb should keep dew points in the low to mid 70s during \npeak heating, particularly on Tuesday and Wednesday, resulting \nin peak heat indices in the 100 to 105 range. \n\nRelief from the heat looks plausible later in the week as a\nbackdoor cold front sags southward into the area. However, this\nlooks to come at the expense of a period of more active weather\n(a period or two of thunderstorms, some possibly strong) at the\nend of the week. The specifics such as the timing of the front\nand best potential for thunderstorms remain a bit unclear at\nthis point, but storm chances begin to ramp up across our area\nlate Wednesday night into Thursday.\n\nKJB",
